<h2>Why the statute is silent and the principles are not</h2>
<p>Neither Section 125 CrPC — now Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — nor Sections 24 and 25 of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, nor Section 20 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005 says anything about tax. The Income-tax Act, 1961 is equally silent on alimony as a category. The treatment therefore rests on the oldest distinction in tax law: revenue receipts are income; capital receipts are not, unless specifically charged. Courts and tribunals examining matrimonial payments have applied exactly that distinction, producing the working rules summarised below.</p>

<h2>The working framework</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Payment</th><th>Generally accepted treatment</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly or periodic maintenance (interim or final)</td><td>Taxable in the recipient\'s hands as income from other sources</td><td>A recurring receipt enjoyed as income is revenue in character</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lump-sum, one-time settlement in lieu of all claims</td><td>Generally not taxable — a capital receipt</td><td>Compensation for extinguishing the right to maintenance, not an income stream</td></tr>
<tr><td>Child maintenance paid to the custodial parent</td><td>Received for the child\'s benefit; parents should take advice on assessment, since the custodial parent holds it for the child</td><td>The payment discharges the child\'s right, not the parent\'s</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payer\'s position</td><td>No deduction in any case</td><td>A personal obligation is not expenditure for earning income</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The edges that need professional advice</h2>
<p>Three situations resist mechanical rules. First, a "lump sum" paid in a handful of instalments: substance governs, and a settlement genuinely extinguishing all claims should retain capital character, but the deed must say so unambiguously. Second, transfers of property — a house, shares, jewellery — as part of a divorce settlement: such transfers raise questions under the gift-taxation provision in Section 56(2)(x) and on capital-gains cost in the recipient\'s later sale, and the answers turn on timing (before or after decree) and documentation; transfers made under a decree or a registered settlement are on materially stronger footing than informal ones. Third, arrears: a consolidated arrears payment of what was always monthly maintenance does not become a capital receipt merely because it arrives in one cheque.</p>

<h2>Drafting with the tax consequence in view</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Settlement deeds and consent terms should: describe a one-time payment expressly as a full and final capital settlement extinguishing all past, present and future maintenance claims; separate child maintenance from spousal maintenance with distinct figures; specify whether property transfers are in discharge of maintenance claims and annex valuations; and, where maintenance remains monthly, alert the recipient to advance-tax obligations so that the first assessment year does not arrive with interest liabilities. A few recitals at drafting cost nothing; re-characterising a receipt before the tax department years later costs a great deal.</p>
</div>

<h2>Interaction with the maintenance courts</h2>
<p>Tax treatment also feeds back into quantum. Because the payer gets no deduction and a recipient of monthly maintenance may bear tax on it, the real value of a monthly figure differs from its face value on both sides — a point parties legitimately raise in negotiation, and one reason lump-sum settlements under Section 25 HMA or in mediation remain attractive: the payer buys certainty, and the recipient receives an amount whose capital character the settled position generally protects. The financial affidavits mandated by <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> already require disclosure of income-tax returns, so the tax picture is before the court in any seriously contested maintenance case.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article states the position in general terms for information. Tax outcomes depend on the precise documents, the assessment history of the parties and amendments in force for the relevant year. Advocate Manish Jha\'s chamber is not a tax advisory; parties structuring settlements should obtain contemporaneous advice from a qualified tax professional.</p>
</div>
